# Copy to runtime.env or set ORCH_CONFIG_FILE to an absolute configuration path.
# runtime.env is SOURCED, so every value here overrides the environment the
# daemon exports. Set only what this host really owns, and keep ORCH_PROVIDER /
# ORCH_SESSION in step with the daemon — a stale value here silently wins.
# Both are left commented on purpose. The launcher already defaults to exactly
# these values, and on a daemon-driven host the daemon passes the provider and
# session it wants per launch (`ORCH_PROVIDER=… ORCH_SESSION=… launch.sh start`)
# — uncommenting them here would override that and make, for example, a Telegram
# /start_codex silently start the other provider.
# ORCH_PROVIDER=codex
# ORCH_SESSION=orchestrator
# One file must supply the launcher and installed watchdog with this complete
# identity tuple. Do not split these values across unit-local defaults.
# ORCH_RUNTIME_DIR=/absolute/path/to/runtime/orchestrator
# ORCH_STATE_DB=/absolute/path/to/runtime/state.db
# ORCH_LAUNCH_SCRIPT=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/launch.sh
# Provider-scoped model pins. Prefer these over the legacy provider-agnostic
# ORCH_MODEL, which applies to whichever provider is selected and therefore
# hands a Codex model to Claude (or the reverse) the moment ORCH_PROVIDER flips.
# Precedence per provider: ORCH_<PROVIDER>_MODEL > ORCH_MODEL > the pin in
# launch.sh (instance/params.yaml: orchestrator.top_model / fallback_model). An
# unpinned launch emits no --model at all and silently takes the account default.
# ORCH_MODEL=
# ORCH_CLAUDE_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# ORCH_CODEX_MODEL=gpt-5.6-sol
# The Telegram /model command WRITES the two provider-scoped keys above (and
# only those two) into runtime.env, so a tier switch survives a relaunch. It is
# deliberately unable to write ORCH_PROVIDER or the provider-agnostic
# ORCH_MODEL: this file is sourced, so ORCH_PROVIDER here would override the
# daemon's per-launch `ORCH_PROVIDER=… launch.sh start` and desynchronise it
# from binding.provider, which decideRelay then rejects as provider_mismatch —
# every orchestrator turn stops reaching Telegram. Hand-editing either key here
# carries that risk yourself. `launch.sh model` prints the resolved state.
# codex-cli's own default is `reasoning effort: none`, which is not adequate for
# orchestrator judgement. Set empty to leave the account default alone.
# ORCH_CODEX_REASONING_EFFORT=high
# SessionStart standing-context load. Codex takes the same hook wire format as
# the Claude harness, so both providers share this one script; launch.sh
# declares it inline with --config plus --dangerously-bypass-hook-trust (without
# that flag codex drops the hook and the orchestrator boots blind).
# ORCH_CODEX_SESSION_HOOK=/absolute/path/to/.claude/hooks/session-load.sh
# Durable singleton lease. The state database must already exist; otherwise
# runtime scripts continue in graceful-degrade mode and log a SKIP reason.
# The same database is the daemon's ONLY mission source: the liveness watchdog
# and /status read the `missions` table written by core/mission-cli.ts. The
# daemon resolves it as INFRA_STATE_DB > ORCH_STATE_DB > <install root>/runtime/
# state.db and opens it read-only, so point every component at one file — a
# daemon reading a different database than the orchestrator writes sees no
# missions and reports `mission: none` forever.
# ORCH_STATE_DB=/absolute/path/to/runtime/state.db
# watchdog.sh is the ONLY renewer of the orchestrator lease, and it renews with
# this same TTL, so it must comfortably outlive the watchdog tick
# (ORCH_WATCHDOG_INTERVAL, default 60s) — MORE than two ticks, or the lease is
# already dead every time the renewer comes back and a healthy session gets
# fenced. watchdog.sh logs `NO-GO reason=lease-ttl-under-tick` when the two knobs
# are set into that trap. The check is `<=`: a TTL of EXACTLY two ticks expires
# at the instant the second tick is due, so any timer jitter puts expiry first.
# The default 180000 is three ticks at the default 60s cadence.
# ORCH_LEASE_TTL_MS=180000
# ORCH_WATCHDOG_INTERVAL is the one cadence knob: watchdog.sh reads it,
# install-watchdog.sh renders the timer from it, and bootstrap/install.sh renders
# bpa-orchestrator-watchdog.timer from it. ORCH_WATCHDOG_INTERVAL_SECONDS is a
# DEPRECATED alias, honoured only because bootstrap/env.template once shipped
# that spelling while nothing read it — the documented cadence silently never
# applied. Prefer the canonical name; it wins when both are set.
# ORCH_WATCHDOG_INTERVAL=60
# ORCH_WATCHDOG_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
# `/done` rest sentinel. The Telegram daemon writes this file on /done and
# deletes it on the next Human directive, and it TELLS the operator the watchdog
# is asleep. watchdog.sh exits immediately while it exists, so the path must be
# the one the daemon writes; leave unset to derive it from TELEGRAM_STATE_DIR
# exactly as daemon/server.ts does.
# ORCH_DONE_SENTINEL=/absolute/path/to/telegram/daemon/runtime/orchestrator-done
# Restart anti-thrash. Every restart costs provider quota and drops the session's
# work, so a tick may only restart if the last one is at least this old. Night is
# local 22:00-07:00, when the operator is asleep and cannot intervene, so
# recovery is deliberately sooner. Restarts (and suppressed restarts) are
# announced on the nudge outbox, never silent.
# ORCH_RESTART_COOLDOWN=1800
# ORCH_RESTART_COOLDOWN_NIGHT=900
# ORCH_RESTART_STATE_FILE=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/watchdog-restart-state
# ORCH_RESTART_FAILURE_ALERT_AFTER=3
# Daemon liveness probe. The daemon owns the Telegram token AND drains the nudge
# outbox, so its death makes every other signal undeliverable. Set EMPTY to
# disable the probe entirely (hermetic test runs do this).
# ORCH_DAEMON_HEALTH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4822/health
# ORCH_LEASE_FILE=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/orchestrator.lease
# ORCH_WORK_DIR=/absolute/path/to/repository
# ORCH_HEARTBEAT_FILE=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator-heartbeat
# Maximum heartbeat/output age. The heartbeat file is written only at turn end,
# so it says "a turn ended" and never "a turn is running"; a single turn longer
# than this is ordinary for the role. watchdog.sh also reads tmux
# `#{window_activity}` (newest pane output in the session) — but neither signal
# can prove a SILENT long turn is alive, so neither may authorize a kill alone.
# ORCH_HEARTBEAT_MAX_AGE=1200
# Positive in-turn liveness pulse. launch.sh starts
# orchestrator-liveness-pulse.sh inside the supervised pane; it re-stamps
# ORCH_LIVENESS_FILE every ORCH_LIVENESS_PULSE_INTERVAL seconds while the
# provider PID exists and stops when it dies. The watchdog kills on a stale
# heartbeat ONLY when this stamp is present AND older than
# ORCH_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE; fresh means alive (however silent), absent means
# ambiguity — alert, never kill. Keep max age comfortably above the pulse
# interval (defaults: 120s vs 30s).
# ORCH_LIVENESS_FILE=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/orchestrator.liveness
# ORCH_LIVENESS_PULSE_INTERVAL=30
# ORCH_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE=120
# All numeric watchdog knobs are validated by the central bounded parser
# (orchestrator/knobs.sh — the documented ranges live in its header). An
# invalid runtime value falls back to the documented default and is logged as
# `invalid-knob`; an invalid install-time cadence refuses to write any unit.
# Git-progress input for the daemon's liveness watchdog (daemon/server.ts
# livenessWatchdogTick). Every ORCH_STALL_WATCHDOG_TICK_MS the daemon resolves
# ORCH_GIT_REF and treats a changed SHA as progress; if neither the tmux pane
# nor this ref has moved for ORCH_STALL_THRESHOLD_MS the operator is told the
# orchestrator stalled. UNSET means no git-progress signal at all — the pane
# scraper is then the only input, which is precisely how this box shipped with
# a permanently silent stall alarm.
#
# Use HEAD, not origin/main: this host has no push credential, so origin/main
# never moves and would report a permanent stall the moment the pane went
# quiet. HEAD advances on every commit the orchestrator lands locally.
# The repo defaults to ORCH_CANONICAL_REPO_PATH (then $HOME/bpa-dev-
# infrastructure); set ORCH_GIT_REPO_PATH only to watch a different checkout.
#
# These four are read by the DAEMON process, not by the shell scripts that
# source this file. They only take effect if this same file is also the
# daemon's systemd EnvironmentFile (on a daemon host, ORCH_CONFIG_FILE and the
# unit's EnvironmentFile must name one path). Setting them in a file only the
# orchestrator sources leaves the watchdog exactly as blind as before.
# EnvironmentFile does no shell expansion, so keep the values literal.
ORCH_GIT_REF=HEAD
# ORCH_GIT_REPO_PATH=/absolute/path/to/repository
# ORCH_STALL_WATCHDOG_TICK_MS=30000
# ORCH_STALL_THRESHOLD_MS=900000
# Human-visible mission-pressure nudges. watchdog.sh is the sole atomic writer
# of this outbox; the Telegram daemon can consume completed replacements.
# NUDGE_OUTBOX_FILE=/absolute/path/to/daemon/outbox/nudges.txt
# FLEET_IDLE_NUDGE_MS=900000
# FLEET_NUDGE_REPEAT_MS=3600000
# NUDGE_RATE_FILE=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/nudge-rate.tsv
# Nightly full local test sweep over ORCH_INSTALL_ROOT (or INSTALL_ROOT, then
# this repository). Red suites are summarized and nudged but do not make the
# systemd tick fail.
# FULL_SUITE_LOG=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/full-suite.log
# FULL_SUITE_NUDGE_REPEAT_MS=21600000
# FULL_SUITE_NUDGE_RATE_FILE=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/full-suite-nudge-rate.tsv
# Comma-separated relative directory prefixes. Invalid values fall back to this
# visible default, which keeps frozen migration reference suites out of runtime.
# FULL_SUITE_EXCLUDE=migration-prep/
# FULL_SUITE_ON_CALENDAR=*-*-* 03:30:00
# Disk pressure is measured for ORCH_INSTALL_ROOT (or INSTALL_ROOT, then repo).
# At DISK_ALERT_PCT the tick RECLAIMS Docker space, re-measures, and escalates to
# the operator only if the filesystem is still at or above DISK_CRITICAL_PCT.
# Detect-and-alert alone was not enough: a full disk truncates files mid-write,
# so the box corrupts itself while the alert sits unread.
# Reclamation is LABEL-SCOPED: it may delete only Docker resources carrying the
# exact ownership label below, i.e. resources this control plane provably
# created (build images with `--label pro.bpa.owner=bpa-dev-infrastructure`).
# Dangling images are pruned with that label filter; the BuildKit build cache
# is never pruned unattended (it cannot be label-filtered, so any prune would
# be host-global); foreign resources are alert-only. It never prunes volumes
# (operator data), never prunes networks, never stops or removes a container,
# and never passes `-a` or `-f` to an image removal.
# ORCH_INSTALL_ROOT=/absolute/path/to/installation
# DISK_ALERT_PCT=80
# DISK_CRITICAL_PCT=88
# Set 0 to keep the alert and skip all reclamation.
# DOCKER_PRUNE_ENABLED=1
# ORCH_DOCKER_OWNER_LABEL=pro.bpa.owner=bpa-dev-infrastructure
# Opt-in sweep of DISPOSABLE TAGGED images, which are not dangling and so are
# never reclaimed by `image prune`. EMPTY by default on purpose: name your own
# tag convention here or nothing is swept. The pattern only NARROWS the owner
# label above — an unlabeled image never matches, whatever its tag. Keeps the
# newest DOCKER_STALE_TAG_KEEP matches per repository; an image any container
# still references is retained (the removal is never forced) and logged with
# the daemon's reason.
# DOCKER_STALE_TAG_PATTERN=:(rollback|release|nightly)
# DOCKER_STALE_TAG_KEEP=3
# Auth preflight credential stores (read-only, structural checks; launch is
# refused unless they prove subscription login). Override only in tests.
# ORCH_CODEX_AUTH_FILE=~/.codex/auth.json
# ORCH_CLAUDE_CRED_FILE=~/.claude/.credentials.json
# DECISIONS_FILE=/absolute/path/to/pending-decisions.md
# Turn-end delivery override. Leave UNSET: unset means the hook pipes the raw
# provider payload through daemon/relay.ts, which normalizes it and POSTs it to
# /orchestrator/turn-end. Setting this URL sends the RAW, un-normalized payload
# instead, and no daemon route accepts that: /notify requires {"text":…} and
# answers a Codex notify payload with HTTP 400, while /orchestrator/turn-end
# expects the already-normalized record. Point it only at a sink that speaks the
# raw provider format. (It used to read `.../notify` here, which cost the
# watchdog its liveness signal — see orchestrator-turnend-relay.sh.)
# ORCH_RELAY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9999/raw-provider-payload-sink
# Two-way Telegram MCP channel for the claude provider. launch.sh generates a
# secret-free config into ORCH_RUNTIME_DIR and passes it via --mcp-config, so
# the orchestrator can call reply()/status_update() instead of relying only on
# the one-way Stop-hook relay. The daemon serves a legacy-SSE MCP transport
# (GET /sse + POST /message), so the client entry is {"type":"sse","url":...}.
# Set ORCH_CLAUDE_MCP_URL empty to disable the channel entirely.
# TELEGRAM_DAEMON_PORT=4822
# ORCH_CLAUDE_MCP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4822/sse
# The server name is the mcp__<name>__<tool> prefix Claude resolves. It MUST
# match the reply-routing hint the daemon appends to every inbound message
# (mcp__telegram-daemon__reply); a mismatch silently degrades the orchestrator
# back to the one-way Stop-hook relay. Locked by claude-mcp-channel.test.sh.
# ORCH_CLAUDE_MCP_SERVER_NAME=telegram-daemon
# Claude stalls on an interactive trust prompt (never connecting MCP) if the work
# dir was never accepted. launch.sh fails loudly on that; set to 1 to bypass.
# ORCH_SKIP_TRUST_CHECK=0
# ORCH_CLAUDE_MCP_CONFIG=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/claude-mcp-config.json
# Morning readiness report. The Telegram daemon watches this path and sends each
# completed file silently; the orchestrator only writes the digest atomically.
# MORNING_OUTBOX_FILE=/absolute/path/to/daemon/outbox/morning.txt
# MORNING_WATERMARK_FILE=/absolute/path/to/orchestrator/runtime/morning.watermark
